# Living Survey: Quantum_Computing                                                                                   
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
## Introduction                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
This document collects the scientific literature regarding **Quantum_Computing**.                                    
                                                                                                                     

I will add sections:                                                                                                 


Quantum Software Ecosystems and Algorithmic Optimization                                                             

Recent studies have focused on the practical deployment and optimization of quantum algorithms. An empirical         
investigation into developer discussions on Stack Overflow reveals that hybrid quantum-classical computing and       
circuit implementation are the most prevalent topics, with Qiskit and Q-sharp dominating the ecosystem alongside     
frequent references to Grover's and Shor's algorithms [^https:__openalex.org_W7155247427]. In the realm of           
optimization, hybrid granular computing approaches have been shown to statistically enhance evolutionary algorithms  
on current IBM Q processors, addressing the limitations of qubit counts in continuous optimization problems          
[^10.1007_s41066-022-00348-9]. Furthermore, specialized software like PULSEE has emerged to simulate nuclear spin    
ensemble evolution, bridging quantum information science with magnetic resonance experiments for investigating       
emergent quantum phases in materials [^10.1016_j.cpc.2022.108598]. Quantum annealing is also being practically       
applied to configurational analysis, providing a guide for translating chemical structures into qubit representations
for D-Wave processors [^10.1063_5.0151346].                                                                          


Quantum Cryptography and Secure Communication                                                                        

The intersection of quantum mechanics and cybersecurity continues to yield significant advancements. A detailed      
simulation of the BB84 quantum key distribution (QKD) protocol has been implemented to model state preparation,      
eavesdropping attacks, and classical post-processing, effectively bridging theoretical quantum mechanics with        
practical secure communication [^10.6084_m9.figshare.32051724]. This aligns with broader research into quantum       
cryptography, including analyses of BB84, B92, and Shor's algorithm implementations on quantum computers             
[^https:__openalex.org_W7112574406]. The concept of the Quantum Internet is also gaining traction, promising         
ultra-secure connectivity and a network of quantum computers capable of transmitting information encoded in quantum  
states [^10.31695_ijerat.2025.9.1]. On the defensive side, new encryption frameworks like CypherGliph integrate      
dynamic compression and dual authentication, while explicitly benchmarking resistance against Grover’s and Shor’s    
algorithms on classical, GPU, and quantum platforms [^10.56975_tijer.v13i3.161368]. Additionally, cryptanalysis      
reports highlight that improved dual lattice attacks significantly reduce the security margins of post-quantum       
finalists like Kyber and Dilithium, emphasizing the need for rigorous security evaluation [^10.5281_zenodo.6493704]. 


Emerging Hardware Paradigms and Computational Applications                                                           

Beyond software, novel hardware architectures and cross-domain applications are being explored. A unified framework  
proposes "atom computing," where controlled flows of single electrons or photons replace transistors to overcome     
material scaling constraints, positioning itself as an alternative to current qubit-based systems                    
[^10.34257_gjsfra257362]. In engineering, quantum computing is being applied to computational fluid dynamics (CFD)   
for aeronautical heat exchangers, utilizing approaches like the Lattice Boltzmann Method and scientific machine      
learning, though it remains in an early developmental stage [^10.11159_icffts23.133][^10.21741_9781644902813-38].    
Broader comparative analyses continue to examine the architectural differences between digital, quantum, and         
biological computers, providing standardized models for industry adoption [^10.1109_mc.2021.3083430]. Tracking these 
developments, recent analyses of open-source repositories and financial news demonstrate the pivotal role of         
community-driven platforms in monitoring emerging quantum trends [^10.1007_978-3-031-90727-2_20].                    

